## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillatory Motion and Waves)
## DBsection(Energy In Waves: Intensity)
## Date(December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Brandon Lostracco)
## Edited (Connor Wilson, May 1, 2018)
## TitleText1('College Physics')
## AuthorText1('Urone et. al')
## EditionText1('2017')
## Section1('16.11')
## Problem1('7')
## KEYWORDS(waves, power, energy)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$percent   = random(5,  20,  0.1);
$intensity = random(50, 90,  1);
$power     = random(50, 150, 1);

$A1 = ($power / $intensity) / ($percent * 0.01);
$A2 = (($power) / (1000)) * (2) * (365) * (10) * (0.09);

BEGIN_PGML
A photovoltaic array of solar cells is [`[$percent]`][`\textrm{%}`] efficient in converting incident solar energy into electricity.

a)  If the average intensity of sunlight on one day is [`[$intensity]`] [`\textrm{W/m}^2`], what area should your array have to produce electrical energy at the rate of [`[$power]`] [`\textrm{W}`]?

    [_]{$A1}  [`\textrm{m}^2`]

b) What is the maximum cost of the array if it must pay for itself in two years of operation averaging [`10.0`] hours at per day? Assume that it earns money at the rate of [`9.00`] cents per kilowatt-hour.

    $ [_]{$A2} 

END_PGML
BEGIN_PGML_HINT
How many _kilowatt_-hours of electrical energy are the solar cells able to produce in the given time frame? The maximum cost is the _break-even point_ - the point at which the solar cells generate just enough revenue to recoup expenses incurred in their purchase and operation.
END_PGML_HINT

ENDDOCUMENT();
